#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# lcm

#% text
# `lcm(a, b, ...)` gives the least common multiple of integers or rational numbers.

#% text
# `lcm(p, q, ...)` gives the least common multiple of polynomials.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# The result is non-negative; `lcm()` is 1 and a zero argument gives 0.

#% item
# For rationals the lcm is the lcm of the numerators over the gcd of the denominators.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
lcm(4, 6)
#% output
#= 12

#% cell
lcm(2, 3, 5, 7)
#% output
#= 210

#% cell
lcm(1/2, 2/3)
#% output
#= 2

#% cell
lcm(x^2 - 1, x + 1)
#% output
#= -1 + x^2

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# The lcm of a list:

#% cell
apply(lcm, range(20))
#% output
#= 232792560

#% subsection
# Applications

#% text
# When two periodic events of periods 12 and 18 coincide again:

#% cell
lcm(12, 18)
#% output
#= 36

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# `gcd*lcm` is the product for two positive integers:

#% cell
[gcd(84, 120)*lcm(84, 120), 84*120]
#% output
#= [10080, 10080]

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `gcd`, `polynomial_lcm`, `extended_gcd`
